  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through next Thursday)\nIssued at 1211 PM CDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nThe upper ridging will continue to elongate across the southern \nCONUS through early next week. This will keep conditions dry for the \nmuch of the long term forecast. Temperatures remain hot in the upper \n90s to triple digits. Currently, Wednesday looks to be the hottest \nday for the long term forecast. If this trend continues, can expect \nHeat Advisories for future forecasts. At the moment, there is a \nslight chance for thunderstorms for the latter half of Sunday for a \nsmall portion of Parmer County in the far southwestern Texas \nPanhandle. The western edge of the upper ridging will reach eastern \nNM on Sunday. An embedded shortwave will result in thunderstorms \ndeveloping across central to eastern NM. There is a slight chance \nthese storms could sneak into our northwestern corner of the \nforecast area. However, models are in disagreement with this \nevolution. The ECMWF, which tends to have a more wet bias, has \nprecipitation possible across the far southwestern Texas Panhandle \nwhile the GFS, which has a more dry bias, keeps us dry. The models \nshould align better for future forecasts. Check back for updates. An \nupper pattern change later next week could bring another round or \ntwo of thunderstorms with upper ridging amplifying across western \nCONUS while an upper trough swings across the Midwest.",
